import{_ as t,o,c as n,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.1C62pOns.js";const s="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/workflows-detalhe.png",r="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/workflows-fluxo.png",i="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/workflows-codigo.png",l="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/workflows-versoes.png",h="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/execucoes-lista.png",d="/pro-agent-hub-installer/capturas/en/execucoes-detalhe.png",k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Workflows","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/interface/workflows.md","filePath":"en/interface/workflows.md","lastUpdated":1787856241000}'),c={name:"en/interface/workflows.md"};function p(u,e,g,w,f,b){return o(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="workflows" tabindex="-1">Workflows <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workflows&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A <strong>workflow</strong> is an automation: a sequence of steps the platform runs on its own — manually, on a schedule, or called from somewhere else.</p><p>There are two types, chosen by a pair of buttons on the General tab:</p><ul><li><strong>Low-code</strong> — a flow drawn as connected boxes.</li><li><strong>High-code</strong> — a Python file.</li></ul><p>The type can be switched at any time, and <strong>both contents are kept</strong>: turning it into high-code does not erase the drawn flow, and going back to low-code does not erase the code. What changes is which of the two the platform runs — and which tab you see.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">The co-pilot writes both</p><p>The Pegasus button, on the General tab, takes a request in plain language and returns either a flow or the Python — and the platform recognises which came back, switches the type and drops the result on the right tab. It is the shortest path out of a blank screen, and also the way to ask &quot;turn this flow into code&quot;.</p></div><h2 id="general-tab" tabindex="-1">General tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#general-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;General tab&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+s+'" alt="workflows-detalhe"></p><p>Description, group and the <strong>schedule</strong>.</p><p>The schedule is a <strong>cron expression</strong>, evaluated in UTC. The screen shows the upcoming runs computed from the expression — check them there before saving; it is faster than finding out the next day that the time was off.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A scheduled workflow never overlaps itself</p><p>If the time comes round and the previous run is still going, the trigger is <strong>skipped</strong>, and recorded as such. That is the guarantee that a scheduled process never runs twice at once. Manual runs have no such lock.</p></div><p>The <strong>log level</strong> decides how much a run records: errors only, progress, or everything. A detailed log helps you debug and weighs on the database — old runs are pruned by the retention configured in <a href="/pro-agent-hub-installer/en/interface/administracao#settings">Settings</a>.</p><h2 id="flow-tab-low-code" tabindex="-1">Flow tab (low-code) <a class="header-anchor" href="#flow-tab-low-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Flow tab (low-code)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+r+'" alt="workflows-fluxo"></p><p>The canvas where the flow is built. Drag nodes from the palette, wire outputs to inputs, and click a node to configure it.</p><p>The node types cover input and output, condition, loop, calling an agent, team or planner, data and system operations, and a <strong>script</strong> node for whatever does not fit the rest.</p><p><strong>Live execution.</strong> When you run it from the test button, the canvas follows along:</p><ul><li>the running node <strong>pulses, with a highlighted border</strong>;</li><li>when it finishes it changes colour according to the result and shows <strong>how long it took</strong>;</li><li>the ones that have not run yet stay dimmed.</li></ul><p>This happens <strong>during</strong> the run, node by node, not only at the end. On a long flow, it is how you see where it is stuck.</p><h2 id="code-tab-high-code" tabindex="-1">Code tab (high-code) <a class="header-anchor" href="#code-tab-high-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Code tab (high-code)&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+i+'" alt="workflows-codigo"></p><p>A Python editor with autocomplete for the platform functions — type <code>db_</code> and the list appears, with the signature and the description of each one.</p><p>The code runs with a scope already assembled: <code>context</code> (what came in), <code>log()</code>, <code>is_cancelled()</code> and the data, system and integration operations. No <code>import</code> is needed to use them. The full reference is in <a href="/pro-agent-hub-installer/en/funcoes/workflow_highcode">Workflow high-code</a>.</p><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A long workflow should check for cancellation</p><p>Cancellation is <strong>cooperative</strong>: the platform records the request, and it is your code that decides to stop. In a long loop, call <code>is_cancelled()</code> each time round and bail out when it returns true. Without that, cancelling only takes effect when the run ends by itself.</p></div><h2 id="executions-tab" tabindex="-1">Executions tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#executions-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Executions tab&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The history of that workflow, with status, duration and the log of each run.</p><h2 id="versions-tab" tabindex="-1">Versions tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#versions-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Versions tab&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+l+'" alt="workflows-versoes"></p><p>Every save keeps a version of the workflow. The tab lists the versions, lets you <strong>compare</strong> two (in high-code, with a line-by-line diff) and <strong>restore</strong> an earlier one.</p><p>Restoring erases nothing: it creates a new version holding the old content. The way back is never lost.</p><h2 id="executions" tabindex="-1">Executions <a class="header-anchor" href="#executions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Executions {#executions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+h+'" alt="execucoes-lista"></p><p>A screen of its own, under Main: the monitor for <strong>every</strong> run on the platform, not just one workflow&#39;s.</p><p>The filter is by workflow description, status and period. While there is an active run, the list refreshes itself every few seconds — and an indicator at the top shows it is following along.</p><p>The statuses are:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Running</strong></td><td>Going right now.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Completed</strong></td><td>Finished with no error.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Failed</strong></td><td>Finished with an exception. The message is in the detail.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cancelling</strong></td><td>Cancellation requested; waiting for the process to reach a stopping point.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cancelled</strong></td><td>Stopped at the user&#39;s request.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Abandoned</strong></td><td>The process running it stopped responding.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Abandoned</strong> deserves attention: it is not an error in your workflow, it is the process that died (container restarted, machine brought down). The platform marks it that way when a run stops showing signs of life for a few minutes, so no record stays stuck at &quot;running&quot; forever.</p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt="execucoes-detalhe"></p><p>The detail shows the origin (manual, scheduled, webhook or API), who triggered it, the duration, the current node, a preview of the output and the full log.</p><h3 id="cancelling" tabindex="-1">Cancelling <a class="header-anchor" href="#cancelling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cancelling&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The cancel button works <strong>even when the run was triggered by the scheduler</strong>, in another process — the request is written to the database and the process running it reads that within seconds.</p><p>In low-code, cancellation is checked between one node and the next: a node that has already started finishes. In high-code, it depends on your <code>is_cancelled()</code>.</p>',42)])])}const y=t(c,[["render",p]]);export{k as __pageData,y as default};
